{"id":"matching-trajectories","title":"Paths that match the known cases","question":"Whose last four years of City money look most like the four years the known cases went through before they became public?","finding":"This ranks paths for a first look; it is not evidence about any organization. 29 known-case nonprofits have a full four-year record before their matter became public. By the fourth year the middle one was paid 1.43 times its first-year figure (other nonprofits, fiscal 2023 to 2026: 1.03 times); 21% of their money on average was awarded without competition (others: 7%); and monitoring left findings open in 34% of the years they were monitored (others: 12%). The average known-case path and the average ordinary path are 0.76 apart, and only 18 of the 29 known cases sit nearer their fellow cases’ average than the ordinary one, so the path is a weak marker: most known cases did not look unusual on these measures before they became public. 54 of 268 nonprofits in no case file sit nearer the known-case path than the ordinary one; the closest is Shanti Project at 0.33 (its nearest single case: Baker Places Inc).","how":"For each profiled nonprofit and fiscal year: City payments; the share of them keyed to a rule that sets competition aside (emergency, waiver by ordinance, sole source, other “not bid”); the number of kinds of exception that year (those four, a Board-approved amendment, an approval after the fact, a contract starting between $9.5 million and $10 million); and whether the Controller’s monitoring left a finding open. A known case’s path is its four fiscal years before the year the matter became public; everyone else’s is fiscal 2023 to 2026, for nonprofits paid $1 million or more over those years. Four things are compared, each given equal say: growth in payments over year 1 (in doublings, capped at sixteen-fold either way), the share without competition in each year, the exception kinds in each year, and the share of monitored years with an open finding. Each number is divided by its spread across all the nonprofits; the distance between two paths is the root-mean-square difference (0 is identical, 1 is one typical spread apart throughout). Listed are the nonprofits nearer the average known-case path than the average path of the other nonprofits, closest first; the nearest single known case is also given. The check on the measure tests each known case against the average of the other known cases.","limits":"A few dissimilar paths are not a profile of wrongdoing: the known cases include very large long-funded providers and small new ones, and what they share (growth, awards without competition) may simply be what emergency-rule funding looked like in those years. An organization can match because it grew for good reason. The monitoring file has no results for fiscal 2020 or 2021 or after fiscal 2025, so “no open finding” often means “not monitored”. Award-method records begin in fiscal 2019, which leaves out every case that became public before fiscal 2023, and businesses are left out because monitoring does not cover them. Distances are printed to two decimals for sorting, not because they are that exact. The ranking is deliberately not attached to any organization as a warning sign. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"org","t":"Organization (in no case file)","type":"org"},{"k":"distance","t":"Distance from the average known-case path","type":"text"},{"k":"ordinaryDistance","t":"Distance from the average path of other nonprofits","type":"text"},{"k":"nearestCase","t":"Nearest single known case","type":"text"},{"k":"nearestDistance","t":"Distance from it","type":"text"},{"k":"fy2023","t":"Paid fiscal 2023","type":"usd"},{"k":"fy2024","t":"Fiscal 2024","type":"usd"},{"k":"fy2025","t":"Fiscal 2025","type":"usd"},{"k":"fy2026","t":"Fiscal 2026"